WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.360
Speaker 1: I've been telling you for a while
Ron DeSantis is dangerous.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.349
And part of what makes him dangerous is that
he has a lot of the same terrible authoritarian

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:14.830
instincts of Donald Trump, but he is more
able to potentially get things done and less

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.380
cartoonishly unhinged.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.619
Piers Morgan interviewed Ron DeSantis, and
remember that Ron DeSantis is experiencing

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:25.080
significant shrinkage in the polls.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.080
He hasn't actually announced that he's running
yet.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.289
So this is all very speculative and very hypothetical.

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:33.600
But one of the interesting questions that
was asked by Piers Morgan during this interview

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.790
was what are the actual differences between
you and Trump?

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.700
Other Republican candidates and potential
candidates have said there really are no differences

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.579
other than identity.

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:45.990
Nikki Haley said, well, I'm younger and a
woman and Indian.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:50.710
Tim Scott said, I don't know that I have any
policy differences, but here is Ron DeSantis.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:52.350
His answer to that question is.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.770
Speaker 2: What do you think of the differences?

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:56.770
Well, I mean, I think there's a few things.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.790
I mean, obviously, you know, the approach
to COVID was different.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.590
I mean, you know, I would have fired somebody
like Fauci.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:06.310
I think that he got way too big for his britches
and I think he did a lot of damage.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:11.320
I also think just in terms of my approach
to leadership, you know, I get personnel in

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.140
the government who have the agenda of the
people and share our agenda.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.250
If you bring your own agenda in, you're gone.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.430
We're just not going to have that.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.310
So the way we run the government, I think,
is no daily drama.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.790
Focus on the big picture and put points on
the board.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.260
And I think that that's something that's very
important.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:35.369
Speaker 1: So DeSantis is essentially saying
the differences are style, and I would have

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:37.299
fired Fauci.

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:41.570
Not exactly a big gap between him and Donald
Trump.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:47.320
Interestingly enough, Piers Morgan did ask
Ron DeSantis his views on the nicknames that

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.820
Trump has come up for him.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.530
Speaker 2: Is your favorite nickname that
Trump has given you so far?

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.799
Is it Ron, Ronda, sanctimonious or meatball?

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:54.799
Right.

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:55.799
Well, I can't even.

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:56.799
He went off Meatball, right?

00:01:56.799 --> 00:01:59.659
I can't I don't know how to spell the sanctimonious.

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:00.799
I don't really know what it means.

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:02.689
But, you know, I kind of like it's long.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:03.689
It's got a lot of vowels.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:05.399
I mean, so we go with that.

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:06.399
That's fine.

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:07.399
You know?

00:02:07.399 --> 00:02:08.399
Speaker 1: Isn't this fun, guys?

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:09.399
Speaker 2: You call me, you call me whatever
you want.

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:12.030
I mean, just as long as you, you know, also
call me a winner.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.570
Speaker 1: Right, Right.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.600
DeSantis is the winner.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.310
Asked whether he believes if he were to run.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:19.310
Oh, sorry.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.080
No, I want to do Putin next.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.080
The topic of Vladimir Putin came up and this
is very interesting.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.830
Speaker 2: What's your view of Putin?

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.010
Look, I think he's got grand ambitions.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.940
I think he's hostile to the United States.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.940
But I think the thing that we've seen is he
doesn't have the conventional capability to

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.380
realize his ambitions.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.540
And so he's basically a gas station with a
bunch of nuclear weapons.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.989
And so for us, one of the things we could
be doing better is utilizing our own energy

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:45.290
resources in the United States.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:50.580
We could be permitting natural gas pipelines
from Marcellus doing a lot in in Alaska.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.800
That's where he gets all his power.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.800
And obviously, he's influenced Europe by by
having so much energy.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.580
So the way to hit Putin is to hit him with
energy.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:03.730
But I do think you look back at all the defense
analysts and me in the past, we overestimated

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:04.840
his conventional capability.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.560
This has been a huge blunder for him, huge
costs.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.050
And, you know, we'll see what ends up happening
with his longevity in power.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.130
But but this has been a this has been a loss
for them.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:20.360
There is a move now to hold him accountable
for war crimes, bombing, maternity hospitals

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:26.090
and genocidal activity in parts of Ukraine,
wiping out whole cities, Mariupol and others.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.090
Would you support that?

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.530
I mean, I think he is a war criminal.

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:34.680
This ICC, we have not done that in the United
States because we're concerned about our soldiers

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.720
or people being brought under it.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.360
But I do think that that he should be held
accountable.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:44.760
Speaker 1: So up sort of sounds like tough
talk, but a lot of the same caveats that many

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.650
